+## Technical foundations with primary citations
+
+### OWL 2 RL and rule-based reasoning
+
+OWL 2 defines multiple profiles designed for specific reasoning tradeoffs; OWL 2 RL is the profile intended for scalable rule-based implementation. citeturn15search19 The practical interpretation for beep-effect is: represent code facts as RDF-like triples (or an isomorphic property-graph encoding) and apply a forward-chaining rule set to derive useful implied facts (inheritance propagation, dependency abstraction, alias/canonicalization, inverse edges, etc.).
+
+Complexity results help motivate tractability constraints. ter Horst’s Journal of Web Semantics paper proves that RDFS entailment is decidable, NP-complete in general, and **in P when the target graph contains no blank nodes**, a useful lens when choosing grounded identifiers for code elements. citeturn15search0
+
+### Incremental materialization maintenance
+
+In a documentation system, code changes are continuous; re-reasoning from scratch is expensive. Incremental datalog materialization is therefore a natural fit.
+
+- DRed (“Delete and Rederive”) originates in the materialized view maintenance literature and is classically associated with Gupta, Mumick, and Subrahmanian’s SIGMOD work. citeturn8search21turn8search6  
+- Motik et al. propose the Backward/Forward (B/F) algorithm for incremental datalog materialization, reporting large performance advantages vs DRed in some settings. citeturn8search1turn8search4
+
+For an engineer-facing white paper, the key point is architectural: **incremental inference is a first-class requirement** for IDE/CI integration, and existing algorithms offer known tradeoffs between over-deletion cost and re-derivation cost. citeturn8search1turn8search6
+
+### Efficient equality handling via rewriting
+
+Code graphs frequently contain aliasing: re-exports, barrel files, renamed imports, and symbol duplication across build outputs. OWL’s `sameAs` semantics can cause combinatorial blowups if naïvely materialized. Motik et al. analyze correctness and efficiency issues in `owl:sameAs` rewriting and propose algorithms to support equality efficiently in materialization-based OWL 2 RL systems. citeturn9search1turn9search14
+
+In beep-effect terms: implement **canonical identifiers** for equivalent symbols; store only one representative as the join key for reasoner rules; and keep provenance for “alias edges” for traceability.
+
+### Grothendieck fibrations as schema–instance factoring
+
+The original draft uses category-theoretic language to explain how tag metadata (schema) and tag payloads (instances) relate. That framing can be retained, but the white paper should (a) keep it concise in the main narrative and (b) move deeper proofs/derivations to an appendix, as requested.
+
+A minimal, engineer-friendly statement is:
+
+- A documentation tag system is naturally modeled as a **family of payload types indexed by a tag definition** (“for each tag name, what is the payload schema?”).
+- Dependent type theory motivates this as a dependent sum / “tag + payload” construction; fibrations are one categorical semantics of type families. citeturn10search0turn10search21
+
+This is valuable as a design justification for why “tag definitions” should be first-class and why schemas should be generated from tag metadata rather than handwritten repeatedly.
+
+## System architecture and implementation tradeoffs
+
+This section is written as “decision record” content: what you would include in a white paper to help contributors understand constraints and why design choices were made.
+
+### Graph store options
+
+beep-effect’s graph backend choice is not purely about raw speed; it must support: incremental updates, multi-tenant or multi-project isolation, query ergonomics, and (ideally) rule-based inference or easy integration for it.
+
+image_group{"layout":"carousel","aspect_ratio":"16:9","query":["property graph database architecture Neo4j Cypher","RDFox datalog materialization engine diagram","GraphBLAS sparse matrix graph database FalkorDB architecture"],"num_per_query":1}
+
+**FalkorDB**: FalkorDB describes itself as a graph database that uses **GraphBLAS** and sparse adjacency matrices under the hood. citeturn23search0turn23search16 It supports Cypher/OpenCypher-style querying (with documented Cypher coverage pages). citeturn23search3turn23search7  
+- **Strengths**: Fan-out heavy graph workloads can be fast; FalkorDB publishes benchmark claims showing sub-140ms p99 latency and large differences vs Neo4j on specific aggregate expansion workloads. These are vendor-reported and should be treated as such in the white paper. citeturn23search1turn23search4  
+- **Risks**: Reasoning is not described as built-in RDFS/OWL; inference must be implemented at the application layer (or via external materialization). (This is not a negative; it is simply an engineering scope item.)
+
+**Neo4j**: Neo4j provides ACID transactional behavior; its documentation explicitly describes Neo4j as ACID compliant in transaction execution semantics. citeturn23search2turn23search6  
+- **Strengths**: Mature tooling, transactions, operational ecosystem, and a broad Cypher user base.  
+- **Risks**: Performance for specific high-fanout analytical workloads may differ; do not repeat vendor-vs-vendor benchmark numbers as universal truth without specifying workload and reproduction.
+
+**RDFox**: RDFox is explicitly designed as a main-memory RDF store supporting **materialization-based parallel datalog reasoning** and SPARQL query answering. citeturn8search2turn8search11 Its published evaluation reports high-scale triple storage and high throughput under their experimental setup. citeturn8search8turn8search11  
+- **Strengths**: “Reasoner + store” integration is exactly aligned with OWL 2 RL / datalog-style reasoning.  
+- **Risks**: Integration complexity and licensing/deployment constraints may be higher than using a property-graph store; engineering cost can dominate for early prototypes.
+
+**Recommended posture for the white paper**: treat the database as a pluggable backend; specify the KG/inference interface and incremental update contract as the stable boundary, and document any store-specific optimization as optional.
+
+### Protocol and service architecture
+
+For tool integration in IDEs and agent environments, the Model Context Protocol (MCP) provides a structured way to expose tools/resources/prompts over a JSON-RPC style protocol. Anthropic’s MCP announcement describes the protocol as an open standard for connecting AI systems to tools and data sources, and the MCP specification codifies protocol requirements. citeturn11search3turn11search6
+
+For a TypeScript implementation, an Effect-based architecture can help enforce explicit dependencies and deterministic wiring. Effect documentation describes services/layers as a disciplined dependency-injection pattern and documents schemas via `Schema.Class`. citeturn19search0turn19search1turn19search11 This can be framed as an implementation detail: contributors do not need to love Effect to evaluate beep-effect’s novelty, but it can reduce incidental complexity in a multi-service pipeline.
+
+### Incremental reasoning and update design
+
+Reasoning must respond to file changes quickly enough for editor workflows. The white paper should propose:
+
+- **Named-graph scoping** by file/module: rebuild only the changed file’s triples/edges and affected neighborhoods.  
+- **Incremental materialization** using techniques inspired by DRed/B/F. citeturn8search6turn8search1  
+- **Equality rewriting layer** for alias canonicalization (re-exports, renames). citeturn9search1turn9search14  
+
+Risks and mitigation should be explicit: if incremental updates are too complex initially, start with rebuild-on-change for small repos and graduate to incremental maintenance once correctness is validated.
+
+## Pipeline, De‑Hallucinator, and provider constraints
+
+### End-to-end pipeline
+
+The pipeline below is the “center diagram” expected in a white paper. It makes the deterministic-first and verification loop concrete.
+
+```mermaid
+flowchart LR
+  A[Codebase] --> B[Layer 1: Syntax parse<br/>tree-sitter]
+  A --> C[Layer 2: Type-aware analysis<br/>TypeScript checker / ts-morph]
+  B --> D[Code fact extractor]
+  C --> D
+  D --> E[Knowledge Graph + Rules<br/>RDFS/OWL RL-style]
+  E --> F[Context builder<br/>2-hop ego network]
+  F --> G[Schema builder<br/>tag metadata -> JSON Schema]
+  G --> H[LLM enrichment<br/>semantic-only fields]
+  H --> I[De-Hallucinator<br/>claim extraction + verification]
+  I -->|pass| J[JSDoc emitter<br/>AST write-back]
+  I -->|fail| K[Retry policy / Human review queue]
+```
+
+Tree-sitter supports incremental parsing and efficient syntax tree updates, and ts-morph provides a practical wrapper around the TypeScript compiler API for navigation/manipulation and type-aware extraction. citeturn20search0turn19search2
+
+### Entity model sketch
+
+This is a lightweight entity/relationship picture suitable for the main narrative. (Deep ontology details can go to an appendix.)
+
+```mermaid
+erDiagram
+  MODULE ||--o{ CLASS : contains
+  MODULE ||--o{ FUNCTION : contains
+  CLASS  ||--o{ METHOD : contains
+  FUNCTION ||--o{ PARAMETER : has
+  METHOD ||--o{ PARAMETER : has
+  FUNCTION }o--o{ FUNCTION : calls
+  MODULE }o--o{ MODULE : imports
+
+  FUNCTION ||--o{ JSDOC_BLOCK : documented_by
+  METHOD   ||--o{ JSDOC_BLOCK : documented_by
+  JSDOC_BLOCK ||--o{ JSDOC_TAG : has
+  JSDOC_TAG }o--|| TAG_DEFINITION : instance_of
+```
+
+### Claim decomposition and verification
+
+The De‑Hallucinator should be described as derived from established “atomic claim” evaluation lines of work:
+
+- FActScore decomposes long-form text into atomic facts and checks them against a knowledge source. citeturn6search10  
+- SAFE adds search-augmented verification for open-domain factuality. citeturn6search7  
+- VeriScore emphasizes evaluating only verifiable content. citeturn7search8  
+- Claimify improves claim extraction by selection/disambiguation/decomposition with ambiguity handling. citeturn7search1  
+
+In beep-effect, the “knowledge source” is primarily the code-derived KG and static analysis artifacts (call graph, type checker facts). The white paper should define three verification tiers:
+
+- **AST-verifiable**: signature facts (names, types, return types) must match deterministic extraction (expected near-100% precision).  
+- **Graph-verifiable**: claims about direct relationships (“calls X”, “throws Y”) must match KG edges or typed analysis.  
+- **Heuristic/unverifiable**: intent-level claims (“optimized”, “safe”, “best-effort”) must be flagged as low-confidence or require human review.
+
+This design directly targets observed hallucination classes in code generation (naming/resource errors) and supply-chain risks (package hallucinations). citeturn6search4turn6search5turn6search17
+
+### Retry/circuit-breaker and human-in-loop policy
+
+Provider failures and schema brittleness are normal; the system needs policy:
+
+- A bounded retry sequence where each retry reduces degrees of freedom (e.g., fewer free-text fields, smaller context window, stronger instruction to abstain).  
+- A circuit breaker that fails fast during provider outages to avoid cascading CI failures.  
+- A human review queue that surfaces *only* low-confidence or unverifiable claims, prioritizing by risk (public API surface > internal methods).
+
+This policy should be stated as a governance mechanism, not as a promise of perfection.
+
+### Embeddings and retrieval
+
+If the system uses embedding-based retrieval as part of context building, Voyage AI’s `voyage-code-3` is a plausible choice with well-documented properties: the Voyage blog states a 32K context length, Matryoshka-style dimension flexibility (2048/1024/512/256), and multiple quantization formats. citeturn22search0turn22search4 This can be used to implement function/class/module-level retrieval for similarity, but the white paper should emphasize that **KG edges** are the authoritative structural context; embeddings are an auxiliary retrieval channel.
+
+### Structured-output constraints and the `toAgentOutputSchema` workaround
+
+Provider schema subsets materially affect how you design “structured output schemas.”
+
+**OpenAI Structured Outputs**: OpenAI’s documentation requires `additionalProperties: false` in objects, and states that when using Structured Outputs “all fields must be required,” with optionality emulated via union-with-null. citeturn14view0turn14view1 The docs also enumerate unsupported JSON Schema keywords (e.g., `allOf`, `if/then/else`, and—depending on settings—string/number constraints). citeturn14view1turn14view2
+
+**Anthropic Structured Outputs**: Anthropic’s docs define strict-schema limits, including a hard limit of **24 optional parameters** across all strict tool schemas and JSON output schemas. citeturn11search2
+
+These constraints justify a transformation like `toAgentOutputSchema`: convert rich union/discriminated schemas into a “product-of-optionals” schema with a discriminator and nullable fields, so the output remains representable in strict modes across providers. The white paper should present this as a compatibility layer with explicit tradeoffs: more verbose schemas, but higher provider portability and fewer schema compilation failures.
+
+## Evaluation plan and recommended white paper structure
+
+### Evaluation goals
+
+The evaluation section should not only claim novelty; it should demonstrate it via:
+
+- **Ablations**: remove one pillar at a time and quantify degradation.  
+- **Baselines**: compare against representative doc tools (e.g., “LLM-only docstring generator,” RepoAgent-style pipeline, deterministic-only TypeDoc reference extraction). citeturn5search1turn16search16  
+- **Reproducible corpora**: select open-source TS repos across domains; define stable evaluation slices (public API symbols, changed files, etc.).
+
+### Proposed metrics and experiments
+
+| Metric | What it measures | How to compute | Why it supports “four pillars” |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Signature correctness | Parameter names/types/return types match compiler truth | Compare emitted JSDoc vs TypeScript checker output | Validates deterministic-first + schema constraint |
+| Claim factuality (verifiable) | Correctness of verifiable behavioral/relationship claims | Label a sample; compute precision/recall of verifier | Validates closed-loop verification |
+| Hallucination rate | Frequency of unsupported claims in docs | Count verifier-failed claims per 1k symbols | Connects to hallucination literature citeturn6search4turn6search5 |
+| Retry yield | % of failures corrected within N retries | Track pass/fail across retries | Tests robustness of circuit-breaker policy |
+| Incremental update latency | Time from code edit → updated docs | Measure end-to-end in editor/CI | Tests practicality of incremental reasoning |
+| Developer preference | Human utility / readability | Blind review of doc diffs | Validates “semantic enrichment” value |
+
+A strong “novelty demonstration” experiment is a **4-way ablation study**:
+
+- Full system  
+- Minus KG reasoning (replace with RAG-only context retrieval)  
+- Minus structured schema (free-form docstrings)  
+- Minus deterministic-first (allow model to fill structure)  
+- Minus verification (accept first-pass output)  
+
+If the hypothesis is correct, removing deterministic-first and verification should sharply increase signature/claim errors; removing KG reasoning should reduce correctness for cross-file contracts; removing structured schemas should increase formatting failures and reduce edit determinism. The papers discussed in earlier sections provide motivation for why these failures occur and what categories to measure. citeturn6search4turn6search10turn5search2
+
+### Recommended white paper structure
+
+To keep the main narrative readable for engineers while preserving rigor:
+
+- **Main narrative**: problem statement; pillar definitions; system overview; competitive checklist; implementation tradeoffs; evaluation plan.  
+- **Appendices**:  
+  - Category-theory and fibrations (formal definitions, proofs, deeper semantics). citeturn10search0turn10search21  
+  - Formal rule sets and OWL 2 RL mapping notes. citeturn15search19turn15search0  
+  - Reproducibility details (benchmark harness, datasets, scoring scripts).
